\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces The coset diffusion law, panel by panel. (a) $D_\theta \propto T$ at five frictions; (b) $D_\theta \propto (2-\gamma )/\gamma $ at five temperatures; (c) the ratio $\hat  D_\theta /D_\theta ^{\rm  pred}$ over all $25$ cells, $1.0068\pm 0.0219$ with the band drawn. \emph  {Multi-seed status: one trained designed-$SO(2)$ checkpoint (seed 44), the single checkpoint on which the $25$-cell grid was run; the five-seed statements of this appendix are the sign-flip and latch entries, not this grid.} \texttt  {langevin\_noise="fdt"}, Newtonian kinetic mode, $\Delta =0.5$ rad. \emph  {Designed testbed: verification.}}}{8}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:dlaw}{{2}{8}{The coset diffusion law, panel by panel. (a) $D_\theta \propto T$ at five frictions; (b) $D_\theta \propto (2-\gamma )/\gamma $ at five temperatures; (c) the ratio $\hat D_\theta /D_\theta ^{\rm pred}$ over all $25$ cells, $1.0068\pm 0.0219$ with the band drawn. \emph {Multi-seed status: one trained designed-$SO(2)$ checkpoint (seed 44), the single checkpoint on which the $25$-cell grid was run; the five-seed statements of this appendix are the sign-flip and latch entries, not this grid.} \texttt {langevin\_noise="fdt"}, Newtonian kinetic mode, $\Delta =0.5$ rad. \emph {Designed testbed: verification.}}{figure.2}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces The sign flip, and the lever that does erase. (a) The flat coset's half-life \emph  {rises} with friction at every temperature, while the underdamped massive mode's falls --- the two stored directions respond to the dial with opposite sign, with $\gamma _{\rm  crit}=2\varepsilon \mu $ marked. (b) Temperature is the forgetting lever, $n_{1/2}\propto 1/T$ at every friction. \emph  {Multi-seed status: one trained designed-$SO(2)$ checkpoint (seed 44); the $10/10$ seed $\times $ temperature statement and the $+0.9552\pm 0.0422$ slope are this appendix's five-seed numbers, not this figure's.} \texttt  {langevin\_noise="fdt"}, Newtonian kinetic mode, $\Delta =0.5$ rad. \emph  {Designed testbed: verification.}}}{8}{figure.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:signflip}{{3}{8}{The sign flip, and the lever that does erase. (a) The flat coset's half-life \emph {rises} with friction at every temperature, while the underdamped massive mode's falls --- the two stored directions respond to the dial with opposite sign, with $\gamma _{\rm crit}=2\varepsilon \mu $ marked. (b) Temperature is the forgetting lever, $n_{1/2}\propto 1/T$ at every friction. \emph {Multi-seed status: one trained designed-$SO(2)$ checkpoint (seed 44); the $10/10$ seed $\times $ temperature statement and the $+0.9552\pm 0.0422$ slope are this appendix's five-seed numbers, not this figure's.} \texttt {langevin\_noise="fdt"}, Newtonian kinetic mode, $\Delta =0.5$ rad. \emph {Designed testbed: verification.}}{figure.3}{}}
\def\@LN@column{1}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces The two regimes, read off the exact one-step Jacobian at the vacuum. (a) The massive radial mode is non-monotone in $\gamma $, each seed's minimum sitting at its own $\gamma _{\rm  crit}=2\varepsilon \mu $ (dotted), against the dashed underdamped envelope $2\ln 2/\gamma $ drawn on the same axes. (b) The flat mode holds $|\lambda |=1$ at every $\gamma $ to the float64 floor: friction cannot erase the latch. \emph  {Five trained designed seeds, $T=0$, dim 4, hidden 64, $\varepsilon =0.05$, laptop-CPU: verification.}}}{9}{figure.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:massiveflat}{{4}{9}{The two regimes, read off the exact one-step Jacobian at the vacuum. (a) The massive radial mode is non-monotone in $\gamma $, each seed's minimum sitting at its own $\gamma _{\rm crit}=2\varepsilon \mu $ (dotted), against the dashed underdamped envelope $2\ln 2/\gamma $ drawn on the same axes. (b) The flat mode holds $|\lambda |=1$ at every $\gamma $ to the float64 floor: friction cannot erase the latch. \emph {Five trained designed seeds, $T=0$, dim 4, hidden 64, $\varepsilon =0.05$, laptop-CPU: verification.}}{figure.4}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces \emph  {The damping optimum, collapsed --- the full-size version of Figure~\ref {fig:collapse}.} The same eight measured curves and the same $\mp 1$ asymptotes, carrying the annotations the main-text version omits: the two-family legend, the $\gamma =\gamma _{\rm  crit}=2\varepsilon \mu $ marker, the $\mu \to 0$ flat-coset callout, and the crimson probe-floor tick on the $\mu ^2$ colourbar ($1.7\times 10^{-12}$). No plotted value differs from Figure~\ref {fig:collapse}. \emph  {(One-step Jacobian; dim 4, hidden 64, $\varepsilon =0.05$, laptop-CPU; \texttt  {langevin\_noise="fdt"}, Newtonian kinetic mode.)}}}{9}{figure.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:collapse-full}{{5}{9}{\emph {The damping optimum, collapsed --- the full-size version of Figure~\ref {fig:collapse}.} The same eight measured curves and the same $\mp 1$ asymptotes, carrying the annotations the main-text version omits: the two-family legend, the $\gamma =\gamma _{\rm crit}=2\varepsilon \mu $ marker, the $\mu \to 0$ flat-coset callout, and the crimson probe-floor tick on the $\mu ^2$ colourbar ($1.7\times 10^{-12}$). No plotted value differs from Figure~\ref {fig:collapse}. \emph {(One-step Jacobian; dim 4, hidden 64, $\varepsilon =0.05$, laptop-CPU; \texttt {langevin\_noise="fdt"}, Newtonian kinetic mode.)}}{figure.5}{}}
\def\@LN@column{1}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces The shape claims on all four instruments ($\delta =0.05$ rad, 48 $\gamma $ values, 3 emergent seeds). The rollout rate instrument reproduces the Jacobian; both threshold instruments fail below $\gamma _{\rm  crit}$, so no rollout $n_{1/2}$ is ever quoted from a threshold there.}}{10}{table.2}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces The instrument gap is a level, not a rate: on the overdamped branch ($\gamma \ge 2\gamma _{\rm  crit}$) the decay \emph  {rates} agree while the \emph  {threshold} instrument is offset by a per-checkpoint constant flat in $\gamma $. Seed 44's full-window value ($0.9216\pm 0.0027$) sits $0.0004$ below its pre-registered band because that window still contains a second, faster mode (early-window ratio $0.539$); the window-split diagnostic converges monotonically to $1$ into the asymptote. Cause of the threshold offset, measured: the ring write deposits only a fraction of its amplitude in the slow branch (envelope-fit intercept over $\delta $ = $0.638/0.644/0.327$).}}{10}{table.3}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {C}The friction-hole vault, on both arms}{10}{appendix.C}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{app:vault}{{C}{10}{The friction-hole vault, on both arms}{appendix.C}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces The emergent V-curve un-collapsed, and the negative beside it. (a) The $T=0$ half-life against friction per emergent seed from the one-step Jacobian, each with its own $\gamma _{\rm  crit}=2\varepsilon \mu _{\rm  soft}$ dotted; the designed coset has no finite half-life at any $\gamma $ and is drawn at the axis top. (b) $\big  ||\lambda _{\rm  coset}|-1\big  |$: exact to the float64 floor for the designed symmetry and short by $\sim 10^{-3}$ on every emergent seed, which is the pseudo-Goldstone statement of this section in one panel. \emph  {Three emergent MLP seeds plus a matched designed control: evidence.}}}{11}{figure.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:lambdacoset}{{6}{11}{The emergent V-curve un-collapsed, and the negative beside it. (a) The $T=0$ half-life against friction per emergent seed from the one-step Jacobian, each with its own $\gamma _{\rm crit}=2\varepsilon \mu _{\rm soft}$ dotted; the designed coset has no finite half-life at any $\gamma $ and is drawn at the axis top. (b) $\big ||\lambda _{\rm coset}|-1\big |$: exact to the float64 floor for the designed symmetry and short by $\sim 10^{-3}$ on every emergent seed, which is the pseudo-Goldstone statement of this section in one panel. \emph {Three emergent MLP seeds plus a matched designed control: evidence.}}{figure.6}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces The emergent unit has no continuous coset register. A written coset value $\delta $ is held for $20\,000$ steps at $T=0$, $\gamma =0.05$: on every emergent seed the value relaxes to the washboard minimum and nothing is retained, where the designed checkpoint returns $\delta $ exactly. \emph  {Three emergent MLP seeds plus a matched designed control: evidence.} This is the figure behind the capacity statement of $\approx 1$--$1.6$ bits.}}{11}{figure.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:registercapacity}{{7}{11}{The emergent unit has no continuous coset register. A written coset value $\delta $ is held for $20\,000$ steps at $T=0$, $\gamma =0.05$: on every emergent seed the value relaxes to the washboard minimum and nothing is retained, where the designed checkpoint returns $\delta $ exactly. \emph {Three emergent MLP seeds plus a matched designed control: evidence.} This is the figure behind the capacity statement of $\approx 1$--$1.6$ bits}{figure.7}{}}
\def\@LN@column{1}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces The crossover $T^\star $, and why the raw curve cannot be read directly. (a) The raw excess over the exact-Goldstone law is the same on the matched designed control as on the emergent seeds, so the excess is estimator bias rather than physics. (b) Bias-corrected, the emergent coset joins the exact-Goldstone law above $T^\star \approx 3\times 10^{-3}$, the shaded band being the confinement-protected cells below it. \emph  {Multi-seed status, stated plainly: two emergent seeds and one matched designed control, an $n<3$ cell.} $\Delta =0.5$ rad, $\ell _\theta /\Delta <0.06$, \texttt  {langevin\_noise="fdt"}, Newtonian kinetic mode: evidence.}}{12}{figure.8}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:tstar}{{8}{12}{The crossover $T^\star $, and why the raw curve cannot be read directly. (a) The raw excess over the exact-Goldstone law is the same on the matched designed control as on the emergent seeds, so the excess is estimator bias rather than physics. (b) Bias-corrected, the emergent coset joins the exact-Goldstone law above $T^\star \approx 3\times 10^{-3}$, the shaded band being the confinement-protected cells below it. \emph {Multi-seed status, stated plainly: two emergent seeds and one matched designed control, an $n<3$ cell.} $\Delta =0.5$ rad, $\ell _\theta /\Delta <0.06$, \texttt {langevin\_noise="fdt"}, Newtonian kinetic mode: evidence}{figure.8}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces The V-curve measured a second way. (a) $T=0$ $n_{1/2}(\gamma )$ per emergent seed on the one-step Jacobian (I-J, lines), the rollout envelope-rate instrument (I-R3, circles) and the rollout first-crossing threshold (I-R1, dotted), at $\delta =0.05$ rad. (b) The per-$\gamma $ instrument ratio: the rate ratio sits on $1$, the threshold ratio is offset and flat in $\gamma $ above $\gamma _{\rm  crit}$. (c) The same data in collapsed variables. \emph  {(Emergent MLP, 3 seeds, dim 4, hidden 64, $\varepsilon =0.05$, float64, $T=0$, pre-registered $\Rightarrow $ evidence.)}}}{12}{figure.9}\protected@file@percent }
\def\@LN@column{1}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10}{\ignorespaces The friction hole as a memory vault, on the designed arm. (a) The hole is a refrigerator: the momentum variance follows the absorb-only curve and rejects the coupled-bath prediction, giving $T_{\rm  local}=1.26\times 10^{-4}$ inside against $10^{-3}$ outside. (b) At the same $\gamma _{\rm  eff}$ the diffusion constant differs by $8\times $ between a friction field and a scalar friction, which is the mechanism statement in one panel. (c) The vault factor: $107.77\pm 4.78\times $ from the $\hat  D_\theta $ estimator, the quoted number; the per-seed bars beneath it are raw first-passage ratios, boundary-layer biased on the outside arm, which is why the pooled first-passage reading of $86.97\pm 2.94\times $ is not quoted as the vault. The inset states the $T=0$ control: the hole erases nothing. \emph  {Designed $SO(2)$ with an analytic field, 3 seeds, $T=10^{-3}$, $\Delta =0.5$ rad, \texttt  {langevin\_noise="fdt"}, Newtonian kinetic mode, laptop-CPU: verification.}}}{13}{figure.10}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:vault}{{10}{13}{The friction hole as a memory vault, on the designed arm. (a) The hole is a refrigerator: the momentum variance follows the absorb-only curve and rejects the coupled-bath prediction, giving $T_{\rm local}=1.26\times 10^{-4}$ inside against $10^{-3}$ outside. (b) At the same $\gamma _{\rm eff}$ the diffusion constant differs by $8\times $ between a friction field and a scalar friction, which is the mechanism statement in one panel. (c) The vault factor: $107.77\pm 4.78\times $ from the $\hat D_\theta $ estimator, the quoted number; the per-seed bars beneath it are raw first-passage ratios, boundary-layer biased on the outside arm, which is why the pooled first-passage reading of $86.97\pm 2.94\times $ is not quoted as the vault. The inset states the $T=0$ control: the hole erases nothing. \emph {Designed $SO(2)$ with an analytic field, 3 seeds, $T=10^{-3}$, $\Delta =0.5$ rad, \texttt {langevin\_noise="fdt"}, Newtonian kinetic mode, laptop-CPU: verification.}}{figure.10}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces The refrigerator law transfers exactly to an emergent register: $\mathrm  {Var}(p_i)/(M_iT)$ against the absorb-only prediction, 2048 walkers $\times $ 40 samples, 3 emergent seeds; over all 24 field cells observed/absorb $=0.9998\pm 0.0019$, and at $\gamma _\phi =0.5$ $T_{\rm  local}/T=0.12570$, a $7.955\times $ refrigerator against $7.942$ predicted. The coupled bath predicts $1.0$ everywhere and is measured at $0.2235$.}}{13}{table.4}\protected@file@percent }
\def\@LN@column{1}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces The hole confines an emergent register, and the laundering control shows friction alone does not. $T=4\times 10^{-3}$, 1024 walkers, burn-in $20\,000$--$80\,000$ steps, spread by interquartile range; ``hop'' means $|\theta |>1$ rad. The pre-registered stationary-spread comparison is \emph  {void} here and no in/out spread ratio may be quoted from these checkpoints: the control that must return exactly $1.000$ returns $0.4586\pm 0.1181$.}}{14}{table.5}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {D}Exact store-level deletion: prior art and the measured tables}{14}{appendix.D}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{app:deletion}{{D}{14}{Exact store-level deletion: prior art and the measured tables}{appendix.D}{}}
\def\@LN@column{1}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {11}{\ignorespaces The vault on an emergent register; panels are labelled by the pre-registered prediction each tests. (a) The refrigerator law, $\mathrm  {Var}(p_i)/(M_iT)$ against $\gamma _{\rm  eff}$ for 3 seeds $\times $ 2 temperatures, on the absorb-only curve with the coupled-bath prediction (dashed) rejected. (b) The $\gamma _{\rm  eff}^{-2}$ diffusion law: bounded cells as circles and squares, delocalised cells as crosses, the designed cross-check overlaid. (c) Confinement: stationary spread and hop fraction for no hole, the scalar control and the hole. (d) Same-site first passage; hatched bars are censored lower bounds. \emph  {(Emergent MLP, 3 seeds, $T\in \{4,8\}\times 10^{-3}$, both above $T^\star $, $\Delta =0.5$ rad $\Rightarrow $ evidence.)}}}{15}{figure.11}\protected@file@percent }
\def\@LN@column{1}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces The laundering control that fires. Designed store, shipped two-phase read, no learning anywhere; 8 targets $\times $ 3 seeds, 128 paired worlds per target, 18 amplitude levels. Against an \emph  {exact} adversary a boolean TTL flag inside the same store is as detectable as an $e^{-\lambda t}$ amplitude, so there is no measurable differentiator at the AUC level; the control fails to fire only against a resolution-limited adversary, and that resolution is our own modelling choice --- which is why the main text leads with the retrieval geometry instead.}}{16}{table.6}\protected@file@percent }
